    <h1>ASTA Color Value in Paprika: Complete Technical Guide</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Colour is paprika's job. A buyer specifies it as an ASTA Color Value — but the number is widely
      misunderstood, and a lot of "red" paprika fails in the application because the colour was
      specified without understanding what ASTA actually measures and how fast it fades.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What ASTA Color Value measures</h2>
    <p>
      ASTA Color Value is a numerical measure of the <strong>extractable carotenoid pigments</strong>
      in paprika — chiefly <strong>capsanthin</strong> — that give the spice its red colour. The method
      comes from the American Spice Trade Association (ASTA Method 20.1) and is recognized by the US
      FDA, EU spice regulations and ISO 7541. It is expressed in ASTA units: a paprika graded
      <strong>ASTA 120</strong> or <strong>ASTA 180</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The important negative: ASTA measures <em>colour only</em>. It does not measure flavour or aroma
      (that needs GC-MS volatile analysis), it does not measure heat (that is <a
      href="/guides/asta-colour-and-shu-heat-explained/">SHU</a>, the capsaicinoid side), and it does
      not directly measure freshness — though colour does fade predictably with age.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The pigments behind the number</h2>
    <p>
      Paprika's red comes from a family of oil-soluble carotenoids. The dominant one is
      <strong>capsanthin</strong>, which typically accounts for roughly 30–40% of total carotenoids and
      is essentially unique to the <em>Capsicum</em> genus. This uniqueness matters commercially:
      capsanthin is a chemical marker that laboratories use to distinguish genuine paprika pigment from
      synthetic colorants or adulterated material.
    </p>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #d8c9c0;text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px;">Carotenoid</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Typical share</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Role</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Capsanthin</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">30–40%</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Primary red pigment; authenticity marker</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Capsorubin</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">8–15%</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Deepens red hue disproportionately</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Beta-carotene</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">10–20%</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Orange component</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Zeaxanthin / others</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">5–10% each</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Yellow-orange balance</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A subtle but real point: two paprikas can have the <em>same ASTA value yet a visibly different
      red shade</em>, because the capsanthin-to-capsorubin ratio differs. When precise hue matching
      matters, buyers request an HPLC carotenoid profile or a CIE L*a*b* colour read on top of the
      ASTA number.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How ASTA is measured — Method 20.1</h2>
    <p>
      ASTA Method 20.1 extracts the pigment from a weighed paprika sample into acetone, then measures
      the <strong>absorbance at 460 nm</strong> in a spectrophotometer (a 10 mm cell). The absorbance
      is converted to an ASTA value with a fixed formula:
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>ASTA Color = (Absorbance@460nm × 164 × I + C) ÷ sample weight (g)</code>
    </p>
    <p>
      The related <strong>International Colour (IC)</strong> unit is simply
      <strong>IC = ASTA × 40</strong>. Because the method rests on physical absorbance at a single
      wavelength, it is reproducible — but it is also why two samples can read the same ASTA while
      looking different: absorbance at 460 nm does not capture the full visible spectrum.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Colour grades in practice</h2>
    <p>
      Commercial paprika powder typically spans roughly <strong>ASTA 80–180</strong>. Higher-value
      material reaches <strong>ASTA 180–260</strong>, with a theoretical ceiling near ASTA 340 and the
      best commercial paprikas around ASTA 260–280. The grade you need depends on the application: a
      colour-forward snack seasoning wants a high, stable ASTA, while a blend where paprika is a minor
      note tolerates a lower grade.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Processing effects on colour</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Drying</strong> — slow, low-temperature drying preserves carotenoids; aggressive
      heat degrades them and can brown the material.</li>
      <li><strong>Grinding</strong> — fine grinding increases surface area and speeds oxidation, so
      colour fades faster in fine powders than in whole pods or coarse flakes.</li>
      <li><strong>Maturity</strong> — pigment builds as the fruit ripens; early-harvested fruit is
      paler and lower in ASTA.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why colour fades — and how to slow it</h2>
    <p>
      Carotenoids degrade under <strong>oxygen, light and heat</strong>, and ASTA colour declines
      roughly logarithmically with storage time. The practical consequences for a buyer:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Specify colour <em>and</em> insist on fresh lot turnover — a high-ASTA spec on old stock is
      meaningless.</li>
      <li>Require sealed, light-protective, moisture-barrier packaging.</li>
      <li>Store cool and dark; a "fading" batch is usually a storage problem, not a pigment problem.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      See <a href="/guides/chilli-powder-colour-storage/">chilli powder colour change &amp; storage</a>
      for the storage side in detail.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What a reliable colour specification contains</h2>
    <p>
      A dependable paprika colour spec states the <strong>ASTA window</strong> (not a single number),
      the <strong>method</strong> (ASTA 20.1), and ideally a <strong>CIE L*a*b*</strong> read when hue
      matching matters. A lot COA should show the measured value for the lot you are buying — not a
      range copied from a catalogue. See our
      <a href="/guides/paprika-chilli-buying-guide/">buying guide</a> for the full specification
      checklist, and <a href="/products/paprika-powder/">paprika powder</a> for the product side.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Limitations and common misconceptions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"Higher ASTA always means better paprika."</strong> Not necessarily — flavour and
      aroma are independent of colour, and a very high ASTA can come with a flat flavour.</li>
      <li><strong>"ASTA measures heat."</strong> No — heat is SHU; ASTA is colour. A paprika can be
      high-ASTA and completely non-pungent.</li>
      <li><strong>"One ASTA number is enough."</strong> No — two lots at the same ASTA can differ in
      hue; state the window and, for colour-critical work, the carotenoid profile or L*a*b*.</li>
      <li><strong>"Colour is stable."</strong> No — it fades with oxygen, light, heat and age; storage
      and turnover are part of the specification.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The Author's Take</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Position:</strong> In my view, the most common paprika-buying mistake is chasing the
      highest ASTA number while ignoring flavour, freshness and storage — which is how you end up with
      "bright but bland" paprika.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Reasoning:</strong> Colour is only one of three independent dimensions — heat, colour and
      flavour — and it is the one that degrades fastest in poor storage. A stable ASTA 120 lot with a
      documented profile beats a faded "ASTA 180" that was ground six months ago. Buyers who specify
      colour as a window, tied to a fresh COA and a storage protocol, get consistency; buyers who fixate
      on one headline number get surprises.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Disclosure:</strong> This is my editorial view as a paprika supplier's technical team,
      grounded in how carotenoid pigments actually behave — not a laboratory claim. Treat the ASTA
      ranges above as planning bands and confirm the exact window against a lot COA.
    </p>

